    "final_answer": "I have all the information needed. Let me now verify the most recent paper is in the New Submissions section (rather than a replacement or cross-list). The \"New submissions\" section for math.AT shows papers in ascending order by arXiv ID (which corresponds to submission order). The two primary math.AT papers are:\n- [1] arXiv:2609.28804 - \"On the cobordism groups of O⟨n⟩-manifolds\" (submitted Sept 23, 2026)\n- [2] arXiv:2609.29759 - \"Equivariant persistence topological complexity\" (submitted Sept 24, 2026)\n\nSince arXiv IDs are assigned in submission order and the listing shows them ascending (with the highest ID being the most recent), the most recent primary math.AT paper is arXiv:2609.29759. I have already retrieved its full abstract. Now I can provide the final answer.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n**Most Recent Research Paper in Algebraic Topology (math.AT) on ArXiv:**\n\n📄 **arXiv ID:** 2609.29759\n📚 **Subject:** Algebraic Topology (math.AT) — *Primary category*\n📅 **Submitted:** 24 September 2026\n\n### Title\n**\"Equivariant persistence topological complexity\"**\n\n### Authors\n- **Navnath Daundkar**\n- **Ankur Sarkar**\n- **Bittu Singh**\n\n### Abstract\n*\"We introduce equivariant persistent analogues of Lusternik–Schnirelmann category, topological complexity, cup length, and zero-divisors cup length for persistent spaces with group actions, and establish their stability. In particular, we investigate their behavior under equivariant interleavings and obtain bounds for the corresponding erosion distances. For Vietoris–Rips filtrations of compact metric spaces equipped with compatible group actions, we relate the erosion distances between the resulting equivariant persistent invariants to appropriate equivariant versions of the Gromov-Hausdorff distance.\"*\n\n---\n\n### How I found it\n1. Navigated to arXiv.org and clicked on the **\"Algebraic Topology\"** link under the Mathematics section (corresponding to category `math.AT`).\n2. The listing page (https://arxiv.org/list/math.AT/new) shows new submissions for Friday, 25 September 2026, with 2 primary math.AT entries and 5 cross-list entries.\n3. Identified that arXiv IDs are assigned in submission order (higher ID = more recent). The highest ID among the two **primary** math.AT papers is **2609.29759** (submitted Sept 24, 2026, 13:09 UTC), making it the most recent.\n4. Opened its abstract page to retrieve the full title, authors, and abstract.\n\nNote: While a cross-listed paper (2609.30262, \"Multivariate Quandles as Groupoid Invariants\") has a higher arXiv ID, its *primary* subject is Geometric Topology (math.GT), with Algebraic Topology listed only as a secondary subject — so the paper most appropriately categorized **primarily** under Algebraic Topology is the one above.",
